# Line endings.
#
# Git checks files out with CRLF on Windows, which is right for anything read
# on this machine and wrong for anything executed on the server. A shell
# script with CRLF fails in a way that reads as nonsense -- bash treats the
# carriage return as part of the command and reports `$'\r': command not
# found` -- and a sudoers file with CRLF can make sudo refuse everything,
# which is a bad afternoon on a machine you reach only through sudo.
#
# So everything that lands on Linux keeps LF regardless of the platform it is
# edited on.
deploy/*        text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf

# The data files are read by Python on both platforms and are large; leaving
# them alone avoids rewriting thirty megabytes on checkout.
*.jsonl         -text

# Compressed data is bytes, never text. out/structure-chapters.bin opens with
# half a megabyte of JSON before its zlib blocks, so git's own guess (a NUL in
# the first 8,000 bytes) would call it text, and core.autocrlf would rewrite
# CR LF pairs inside the compressed data on commit -- a corrupt clause
# structure that only shows as a decompress error on the server (2026-10-01).
*.bin           binary
*.gz            binary

# Images are bytes too, wherever they sit. `deploy/* text` above would make a
# PNG in deploy/ text and rewrite its CR LF pairs on commit: the social
# preview card was stored corrupt that way on 2026-10-23, caught by comparing
# the stored blob with the file. Later lines win, so these come after it.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary

# Third-party files are pinned by checksum (app2/VENDORED.md);
# line-ending conversion would change the bytes the checksum names.
app2/static/vendor/**    -text
*.woff2                  binary
